WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.840
I got the blues, the TB blues...

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.540
I got the heebie jeebie TB blues

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:43.520
feeling low down, way down low down, tired of my longtime slowdown,

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:51.299
waiting for that special day, when the Doc can say be on your way.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:01:00.220
I got my bed and time to spend, but time drags
on when there is no end,

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:09.600
blue and low down, way, way down, low down. Aiming for that knockout showdown,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:16.140
building to that special day when the Doc can say be on your way.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.360
[Bill:] Hello Doc.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.400
[Doctor:] Hello Bill. How you doing?

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.600
[Bill:] Oh, fine, just great.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.240
[Doctor:] What's wrong, something bothering you?

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.789
[Bill:] You ought to know this by heart now.
I want to get my clothes, get out of here, go home.

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:47.520
[Doctor:] Is that all?

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.360
[Bill:] Oh, you've heard all this before.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.120
I still want to get married, have a family, earn a living.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:02.420
[Doctor:] Bill, you will be able to do all
of those things, I've told you that before.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.159
[Bill:] Yeah, but when do I start?

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:08.739
[Doctor:] You've started already. You started the day you came here.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.921
Seemed like a long time didn't it, like years instead of weeks.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.400
Remember all the examinations we gave you, all the tests we made?

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.250
They showed how sick you really were.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.400
They showed things nobody could know just from looking at you:

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:35.600
suspicious shadows in your lungs, and in your sputum, germs, TB germs.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.140
They didn't just happen; you got them by direct contact with someone who already had tuberculosis.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.760
Or maybe you got them by contact with objects infected by someone with tuberculosis.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.240
You could pass those germs on to someone else,

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.940
your family for instance, if you had been at home.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.840
When the germs first appear, there may be only a few.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.000
And usually a healthy body will wall them in or kill them.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.720
But sometimes there are too many germs for the body to overcome,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:36.720
or you may become rundown, from too much physical activity, too much work, too much play,

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.820
not enough rest and proper food.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.240
Then the germs multiply and you're sick.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.340
There's no medicine that by itself is a sure thing to cure you.

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:57.369
To get well, you must have rest, proper food, and medical care.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.580
To get well, you must be careful...

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:06.319
to limit the things you do until we know what and how much you can do safely.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.739
[Bill:] It helps to be reminded. Gosh, I want
to get well and stay well.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.160
[Doctor:] You may feel well, but the examinations
and x-rays tell us you're still sick.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.040
You've improved but you still have to stay in bed and rest.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.610
[Bill:] I am resting.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.840
[Doctor:] I'm not so sure of that. You don't
seem able to relax.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.320
You move about too much, and that means you're working.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.300
[Bill:] Working? What do you mean?

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:37.600
[Doctor:] Anything that isn't rest is work.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:45.040
Ever do much in sports, or did you ever stay up late playing cards?

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.240
Remember how tired you got?

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.860
Play is work.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.460
Any activity is work, even walking or studying.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:04.160
Bill, many patients simply don't understand what we mean by work.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:09.620
Too often they leave the hospital too soon, do too much, and break down.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.320
Remember Charlie James?

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.720
[Bill:] Yeah, I hear he's back.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.200
[Doctor:] That's right. It wasn't many months ago Charlie decided

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.390
there was no keeping him in the hospital.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.770
He thought he could take it easy at home and
he did for a while.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.600
He had no regular job but...

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.500
he soon found himself playing with the kids,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.480
you know how it is at home, it's hard to sit around and not do anything.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:42.340
That's what happened to Charlie. He got busy without knowing it, even began getting to bed late.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.320
He didn't realize it was all adding up to too much work for him;

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:53.060
In fact, he just didn't know how much he was doing or could do.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:06:00.060
So, Charlie's back again, back where you were when you first came here.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:06.470
At that time, you remember you didn't do anything but rest, and yet you were tired all the time,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.240
but gradually you got stronger until you could read, write,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.260
get out of bed for your meals, and go to the bathroom.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.680
As you get stronger, we'll allow you to do
more, work more.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.060
[Bill:] Yeah, but what does it all get me?

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:31.940
[Doctor:] Just this, you're getting well.
We know now that you can get well,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.530
but it's a gradual process.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.390
At the rate you've been improving, we should be moving you soon.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:44.000
As a semi-ambulant patient, you'll be able to get around a bit, do more to take care of yourself.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:50.940
You'll have full bathroom privileges...you'll
be allowed more recreational activity, too.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.300
Maybe you'll want to hear some music, or play it.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:07:02.420
[A man strums a banjo.]

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:13.700
There are lots of things you'll be able to do.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.160
Of course, being sick does have its rough spots,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.660
and tuberculosis often produces some tough personal problems.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.660
But there are always ways to work them out when you go about it right,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.660
and the social worker will give you expert guidance

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.160
based on experience and the handling of problems just like yours.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.620
Of course you can read, or even study.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.640
If you want it, the educational therapist is always available for extra help.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:03.250
The occupational therapist will give you simple
work tasks, depending on your treatment needs.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.240
You'll be expected to spend a prescribed amount
of time each day in this activity.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:16.360
None of these simple tasks will train you
for a job, but they will show you,

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.160
and show us, how work agrees with you.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:27.640
Your progress will be followed carefully in a number of ways--by means of x-ray films and a variety of laboratory tests.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:38.320
These will be made as often as necessary to show us just how well you are doing

00:08:38.779 --> 00:08:43.239
under the treatment you are getting.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.020
When your disease has become stabilized, you will be permitted still more activity.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:59.500
You'll be able to leave the ward and go to the main dining room for your meals with the other ambulant patients.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.760
You'll get a deep satisfaction out of doing some of the

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.280
simple things you've been denied so long.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:33.780
[Background music as everyday activities are shown: sitting at a bar with a beer, shooting pool with buddies.]

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:44.540
You'll find yourself walking more. You'll
feel you are beginning to live again.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.750
You, yourself, will realize you are able to do more,

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:51.920
but always based on a day of balanced activity.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:00.560
You'll think about work, a job after you leave us, and you can get help in planning for it.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.400
Some fellows make the mistake of not planning.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.470
Tom Baylor, for instance...used to be one of my patients.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.500
He couldn't be bothered about planning or training.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.760
He wasted his time, figured the future'd take care of itself,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.580
and a soft, high-paid job would just fall into his lap.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.580
But, it didn't work out that way.

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:36.410
Tom couldn't make the grade, no training,
no experience.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:42.380
So he wound up as a laborer on a construction job.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.720
He couldn't take it.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:50.220
Now he's back, worse off than before.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:55.520
We could've helped him plan for training and work he could do, but he wouldn't listen.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:01.890
However, most of our patients do listen; they
know our program of gradually increased activity

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:06.440
is prescribed medically according to each patient's progress.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.020
They know it will get them to the point where their plan will began to pay off.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.300
Most of them listen to us.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:17.940
Are you listening?

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.000
Are you listening?

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.940
[Bill:] Well Doc, what's the good word?

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:46.860
[Doctor:] You've come a long way Bill; you're
a fully ambulant patient as of right now.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:50.440
You once told me you wanted to earn a living
when you got out, right?

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.260
[Bill:] Right.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:55.600
[Doctor:] Can you get a job and stay well?

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.510
[Bill:] I think so.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.060
[Doctor:] But do you know?

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.000
[Bill:] Well, not exactly.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:12.260
[Doctor:] Well, no one knows yet, but we can help you find out if you stick with us,

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.300
for we have the facilities to do it.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:19.500
Shops and classrooms for our patients to complete their treatment,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:25.260
to help us find out how much work they can do.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:35.200
Many of them are planning to work at a trade, work with their hands, make things.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:43.880
Others are planning for a profession, but
no matter what they plan to do to earn a living,

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:50.420
a wide variety of facilities is available
to enable the patients to find out for themselves

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:55.180
what and how much they can do.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:02.180
And there's variety too in the amount of time
they spend here each day.

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:06.480
These two men are working on the same project.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:10.640
Hank will complete his in about five days,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:15.680
but Jim will have to spend two weeks to do the same thing. Why?

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:20.360
We allow Jim to work only two hours a day
now,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:25.120
but we've found that Hank can work four hours a day.

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:28.310
Each of these patients works on an individual time schedule.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:34.280
The amount of time each one spends here is prescribed.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.340
We keep a constant check on condition and progress and allow them

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:45.339
to work more only when they can do so without becoming too tired.

00:13:45.339 --> 00:13:49.419
Many want to keep on working even after their time is up, but they learn quickly

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:55.740
that their work is adjusted to give them a proper balance of daily activity.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:01.660
The work done here is not intended to train
anyone for a particular job;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:09.620
it's to find out the patient's ability to perform and physical capacity to perform.

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.010
This applies to every type of endeavor.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:19.280
Frank Lorenzo, for example, wants to do some sort of work in the machinist field.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.080
He made his plans with the help of vocational guidance.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:30.080
His expressed interest in mechanical work
was borne out by tests for aptitude and interest.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:34.410
They demonstrated that Frank had good coordination.

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:38.820
He chose his field, and his records showed us that he could enter it.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:47.029
So we put him on light mechanics. He's doing
many different things that require precision

00:14:47.029 --> 00:14:49.940
and the use of a great variety of precision instruments.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:56.839
His work involves bending, reaching, lifting,
standing...because the type of job he wants

00:14:56.839 --> 00:15:01.419
will include such things. We know the demands of such jobs

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:07.279
and we schedule his activities so he can approximate those demands now.

00:15:07.279 --> 00:15:11.640
What Frank does here will not train him for
any particular job, but he will be able to

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:16.830
choose any one of many in the machinist's
field. He will go out to a school or on-the-job

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:22.830
training and will know he can learn and do exactly what they teach him.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:29.680
He can become a machinist, get a job, live a full life.

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:36.680
Hugh Fleming plans to be a chemical engineer. His scores on the tests we gave him indicated

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:44.080
special aptitude for math. When he came
to rehabilitation, we started him in two classes,

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:50.100
solid geometry and English, a hour a day for each.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:55.770
Now he studies three hours a day and helps
in the hospital lab two hours a day,

00:15:55.770 --> 00:16:01.050
working toward credit in the school he plans to enter when he leaves us.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:13.940
This sort of classroom work, study, and shop work are only part of the day's activites.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:22.800
They take up only a few hours, and during the other hours of the day there's rest and self-care.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:31.490
And there's still another sort of work, the
sort of thing that rounds out a normal day

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:36.030
for anybody...play, recreation, fun, whatever
you want to call it.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:42.310
In whatever form it takes, it's needed in the right proportion to balance a day's activities.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:17:08.720
[A play is being performed on stage, and the men recovering from TB are seated in rows watching it.]

00:17:11.399 --> 00:17:13.899
It all adds up to a fuller life.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:19.320
All these activities are part of a planned program, carried out here in the hospital

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:24.080
to help build you up to that day when you'll be discharged to your own care.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:29.880
The day you will leave us, knowing you are
well again, that you can choose your own work

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:35.580
and be sure you can do it. The day you can
go home for good.

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:42.511
That day will come for you Bill, if you wait.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:47.820
[Bill:] You make it pretty tough for me, in
a way.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.780
You know I want to leave here right now, don't you?

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:54.860
[Doctor:] Sure, I know.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:18:02.400
[Bill:] You know they are waiting for me, back home. We've all waited a long time.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.450
[Doctor:] I know.

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:13.130
[Bill:] Well, I guess we'll just have to wait
a little longer, but it will be worth it, won't it?

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:25.580
[Man singing:] Well, so long Doc, been nice
seeing you. You're looking as healthy as can be...

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:34.159
you're as strong as a rock and I want
you to know that's just what I want to be, healthy.

00:18:34.159 --> 00:18:37.399
That's just what I want to be.

00:18:37.399 --> 00:18:44.399
Gonna lick TB if it's last thing I do, I'll
do whatever you say.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:52.520
Gonna lick TB, Doc, tell me what to do, I'd like to go home but I will stay if you say so.

00:18:52.529 --> 00:18:55.689
Like to go home but I will stay.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:19:03.560
I got a little girl, she's a'waiting for me, so is my family.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:13.500
Oh, I ain't too smart but there's more things
I know, KO TB Doc, KO TB.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:21.760
Oh, I won't get low and I won't be blue, I'll
do whatever you say.

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:29.500
Gonna lick TB, Doc, tell what me what to do. I'd like to go home but I will stay if you say so,

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.140
like to go home but I will stay.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:38.800
Yes, I'd like to go home but I will stay,
Doc, like to go home but I will stay.

00:19:38.809 --> 00:19:39.830
[The End, A Veterans Administration Medical
Film]

